Fork-specific changes
---------------------

The RoutSurs maintainers hold the copyright only on the fork-specific
changes listed below and on new files added by the fork (this NOTICE,
BRANDING.md, the RoutSurs README and the RoutSurs default configuration).

Branding changes:

  * Makefile: BRAND_NAME/BRAND_ID/BRAND_VERSION variables; /etc/os-release
    generation (NAME/ID/ID_LIKE/VERSION/PRETTY_NAME/BUILDROOT_VERSION);
    release tarball name; help text and the "please configure" message.
  * Config.in: kconfig main menu now reads "RoutSurs <version> Configuration".
  * system/Config.in: default hostname is "routsurs", default system banner is
    "Welcome to RoutSurs".
  * README, SECURITY.md, .b4-config, .github/, .gitlab/issue_templates/,
    docs/manual/manual.adoc and docs/manual/introduction.adoc: project name,
    contacts and links.
  * utils/check-package, utils/generate-cyclonedx and
    support/dependencies/dependencies.sh: user-visible messages and the
    default SBOM project name ("routsurs").
  * docs/website/images/routsurs-logo.png: new RoutSurs logo, used by the
    manual; the original Buildroot logo is kept untouched.
  * configs/routsurs_aarch64_defconfig: new default configuration.
  * board/routsurs/aarch64/: kernel configuration and source hashes copied
    unchanged from the upstream aarch64 virt board, retaining their original
    attribution; these files are not claimed as original RoutSurs work.

Fork-specific removals:

  * .github/workflows/repo-lockdown.yml and .gitlab-ci.yml: upstream-only
    infrastructure (a GitHub PR auto-closer pointing at the Buildroot mailing
    list, and a GitLab CI pipeline using the buildroot.org container registry).
    They cannot work in this repository and would mislead contributors; the
    upstream files remain available in the Buildroot history.

What was deliberately NOT renamed:

  * BR2_* kconfig symbols, directory layout, make targets, package
    infrastructures and the BR2_EXTERNAL mechanism.  They are the stable
    interface of the build engine and are shared with upstream Buildroot.
  * Technical documentation and comments that describe how the Buildroot
    build engine works, and package recipes/patches for third-party software.
  * COPYING, DEVELOPERS, .mailmap, CHANGES and support/legal-info/*.

Keeping those intact is what makes it possible to keep merging upstream
Buildroot releases into RoutSurs, and it keeps the attribution accurate.

